    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Cold air compresses fragrance molecules, reducing their volatility and projection. That is why light citrus colognes seem to disappear so quickly when the thermometer drops.</p><p>Autumn is the golden hour of perfumery because you don't need to jump straight into suffocating heavy winter ouds. The key is finding transitional scents: fragrances that combine lingering summer warmth with the rich, comforting depth of cardamom, cedarwood, and dry amber.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "blog--editorial-text-section",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Late Summer Citrus vs Early Autumn Spices & Woods",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p>In late summer, high heat amplified sharp citrus notes like bergamot, lime, and neroli. These high-volatility compounds created an instant cooling sensation in the sun.</p><p>However, as ambient temperatures drop into the 60s and 50s, those light citrus molecules lose their lift, requiring warmer, resinous fixatives to project effectively.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p>Autumn fragrances lean into transitional notes: cardamom, black pepper, fig, cashmeran, and cedar. These ingredients have substantial molecular weight while retaining a clean, aromatic sparkle.</p><p>They wrap around you like a tailored trench coat, projecting a cozy, inviting warmth that feels completely harmonious with falling leaves and overcast skies.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "blog--narrative-content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>The transitional MVPs: Addiction & Mirage</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>When the temperature drops, <strong>Addiction</strong> (inspired by Baccarat Rouge 540) enters its true element. The amberwood, saffron, and cedar accord cuts through crisp autumn air with crystalline brilliance, lingering on wool scarves and jacket lapels for days.</p><p>For men, <strong>Mirage</strong> offers an exquisite blend of powdery iris, rich cedar, and smooth leather that feels like stepping into a tailored Savile Row atelier.</p><p>Meanwhile, <strong>Fate</strong> (inspired by Chance EDP) provides the ideal feminine balance of pink pepper, patchouli, and vanilla that warms beautifully in cool weather.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>Adjusting your spray count for cooler weather</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>In summer, conservative spraying is mandatory to avoid overwhelming people in high heat. In autumn, cooler air and heavier outerwear absorb more fragrance, allowing you to add an extra spray or two to your pulse points with complete confidence.</p><p>Apply to your chest, neck, and the collar of your coat for a warm scent trail that accompanies you throughout the day.</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
